a defeat for opponents of the  government's rwanda programme at least  in the short term the uk high court says  next tuesday's flight deporting the  first group of asylum seekers to kigali  can go ahead  pro-refugee groups had argued the policy  was unlawful and they were backed in  court on friday by the un refugee agency  the unhcr  she outlined the hopelessness  of the judicial system  in rwanda it doesn't have facilities it  doesn't have lawyers it doesn't have  interpreters it simply doesn't have the  capacity to deal with this but of course  that is not the issue  the fact is that rwanda is unsafe it's a  place where any opposition  to the current government is often  locked up  we don't want to see refugees locked up  we want to see refugees look after  safety  but the judge here said it was important  the home office be able to implement  immigration control decisions he's  allowed the groups to appeal though  that's going to happen on monday  all along the government's accepted that  there would be legal challenges to its  plan while insisting it would soon be  sending people to rwanda but it's still  possible it could face a wider judicial  review including into whether rwanda in  fact is a safe country to send  vulnerable people  the uk and rwanda announced the  agreement back in april the first stages  involved a payment of more than 150  million dollars to the rwandan  government  the british government hopes the scheme  will deter people from crossing the  english channel from france in small  boats last year 28 000 people made the  dangerous journey so far this year more  than 10 000 people have done so but  experts doubt that the rwanda scheme  will change much  the uk government has already  implemented a range of  what have been described as hostile  environment policies towards migrants  and asylum seekers and these have not  deterred  people from crossing borders and on the  contrary reducing legal goals for um for  claiming asylum  increases the the the undocumented rules  for now the flights to rwanda can in  theory begin in the next few weeks  opponents of the rwanda scheme will try  to convince the courts that the entire  policy is unlawful nadim baba al-jazeera  london
 

